WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.387
EF Core migrations are production-impacting changes.

2
00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:09.769
They can alter schema, constraints, indexes, relationships, defaults, permissions, and data shape.

3
00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:19.409
A migration that works locally can still have operational and security consequences when it runs against production data and production permissions.

4
00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:22.795
Schema changes and data transformations need review.

5
00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:37.384
Adding a nullable field, changing an index, altering a relationship, dropping a column, widening a permission-related table, or moving tenant data can affect authorization, auditing, performance, privacy, and rollback plans.

6
00:00:37.384 --> 00:00:41.618
Destructive operations deserve explicit review and communication.

7
00:00:41.618 --> 00:00:43.962
Seed data can be security-sensitive.

8
00:00:43.962 --> 00:00:54.057
Roles, users, permissions, feature flags, default settings, administrative accounts, sample tenants, and development shortcuts should be treated carefully.

9
00:00:54.057 --> 00:01:02.915
Seed logic that is harmless in local development can be risky if it reaches staging or production without environment-specific controls.

10
00:01:02.915 --> 00:01:07.539
Development convenience should be separated from production discipline.

11
00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:16.787
Automatic database creation, broad local permissions, sample credentials, test data, and relaxed constraints may help developers move quickly.

12
00:01:16.787 --> 00:01:27.273
Production migrations need change control, controlled execution identity, review, monitoring, and a plan for what happens if the migration fails partway through.

13
00:01:27.273 --> 00:01:30.530
Permission separation is important for migrations.

14
00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:36.978
The identity that runs schema changes may need rights that the runtime application should not have.

15
00:01:36.978 --> 00:01:44.272
Keeping those responsibilities distinct can reduce the impact of runtime defects and make audit records clearer.

16
00:01:44.272 --> 00:01:49.222
Rollback planning and operational communication are part of secure delivery.

17
00:01:49.222 --> 00:02:00.098
Teams should know whether a migration is reversible, whether data loss is possible, how long it may run, what users may observe, and who is responsible for monitoring.

18
00:02:00.098 --> 00:02:05.830
Database change control belongs in the secure software delivery process, not outside it.
